--- !u!114 &492484908
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 492484904}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: e13ee6c849389f642a5aefd5c4b95e18, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: '::'
  entryPrefab: {fileID: 1511958155545834966, guid: 1e8e7db22032cb54ebc656554b47eb01, type: 3}
  container: {fileID: 492484905}
  entries:
  - Text: '<size=140%><b>Anxiety (somatic)</b></size>

      A psychological condition
      where stress and anxiety can produce strong physical symptoms. Patients may
      describe palpitations, chest tightness, shortness of breath at rest, and dizziness.
      Symptoms often fluctuate and worsen with stress, and can improve with reassurance
      and support.


      <b>Main symptoms:</b> Palpitations; Chest tightness; Shortness
      of breath at rest; Dizziness.'
  - Text: '<size=140%><b>Bacterial pneumonia (mild)</b></size>

      A bacterial
      infection of the lungs. Patients often present with fever, productive cough,
      chest pain (especially when breathing), and shortness of breath. Symptoms may
      worsen over time if untreated. Antibiotics are typically required.


      <b>Main
      symptoms:</b> Fever; Productive cough; Chest pain; Shortness of breath. '
  - Text: '<size=140%><b>Bacterial tonsillitis</b></size>

      A bacterial infection
      of the tonsils and throat. It often causes a severe sore throat, fever, and
      difficulty swallowing, and it commonly occurs without a cough. Symptoms can
      develop fairly quickly. Antibiotics are usually needed.


      <b>Main symptoms:</b>
      Sore throat; Fever; Difficulty swallowing; Absence of cough.'
  - Text: '<size=140%><b>Common cold</b></size>

      A mild viral upper-respiratory
      infection. Symptoms are usually centered around the nose and throat, such as
      congestion, runny nose, sneezing, and a mild cough. Fever is absent or low-grade.
      It resolves on its own with symptomatic treatment.


      <b>Main symptoms:</b>
      Congestion or runny nose; Sneezing; Mild cough; Fever is absent or low-grade.'
  - Text: '<size=140%><b>Dehydration</b></size>

      A non-infectious condition
      caused by insufficient fluid intake or excessive fluid loss. Patients may feel
      dizzy, weak, and have dry mouth, with reduced urine output. If not corrected,
      symptoms can worsen. Fluid replacement is the main treatment.


      <b>Main
      symptoms:</b> Dizziness; Weakness; Dry mouth; Reduced urine output.'
  - Text: '<size=140%><b>Depression (mild)</b></size>

      A psychological condition
      characterized by a gradual decline in mood and motivation. Patients may report
      persistent low mood, fatigue, reduced interest or drive, and sleep changes.
      Symptoms tend to build over time. Supportive counseling and follow-up are important.


      <b>Main
      symptoms:</b> Low mood; Fatigue; Reduced interest or drive; Sleep changes.'
  - Text: '<size=140%><b>Food poisoning</b></size>

      A non-infectious illness
      typically linked to contaminated food. Symptoms often begin suddenly and include
      vomiting, diarrhea, and abdominal pain, usually without fever. Most cases improve
      with time, hydration, and observation, but dehydration can be a concern.


      <b>Main
      symptoms:</b> Vomiting; Diarrhea; Abdominal pain; Absence of fever.'
  - Text: '<size=140%><b>Influenza</b></size>

      A common viral respiratory illness
      that often starts suddenly. Patients typically feel very unwell, with high
      fever, strong body aches, and marked fatigue. A dry cough is frequent. Most
      cases improve with rest and supportive care.


      <b>Main symptoms:</b>
      High fever; Body aches; Fatigue; Dry cough.'
  - Text: '<size=140%><b>Urinary infection</b></size>

      A bacterial infection
      of the bladder. Patients usually report burning or pain when urinating, frequent
      or urgent urination, and lower abdominal discomfort. Fever is typically absent
      or mild. Antibiotics are the standard treatment.


      <b>Main symptoms:</b>
      Burn or pain when urinating; Frequent or urgent urination; Lower abdominal
      discomfort; Mild or absent fever.'
  - Text: '<size=140%><b>Viral gastroenteritis</b></size>

      A viral infection
      of the stomach and intestines. It commonly causes nausea, vomiting, diarrhea,
      and crampy abdominal pain. Onset is often rapid and most patients recover within
      a short time. The main risk is dehydration, so fluids are key.


      <b>Main
      symptoms:</b> Nausea; Vomiting; Diarrhea; Abdominal pain.'
